# Fleet inventory.
#
# LAN IPs are used for the RPC mesh. Tailscale is admin/web only -- never put
# RPC on it: encryption on the per-token hot path is pure loss, and upstream
# explicitly warns against exposing RPC to any network it does not trust.
#
# Fields: <hostname> <lan-ip> <ram_mb> <physical-cores> [ssh-user]
#
# RAM_MB drives --tensor-split and must be MEASURED (`free -m`), not assumed.
# CORES is PHYSICAL cores, not nproc -- measured on node 1, -t 8 is 26% slower
# than -t 4 for generation because SMT siblings contend for the memory pipe.
# Get it with:
#   lscpu -p=Core,Socket | grep -v '^#' | sort -u | wc -l
#
# SSH-USER is the ADMIN login on that node, and it is OPTIONAL. Omitted, it
# defaults to the coordinator's own login name -- which is exactly what every
# script assumed implicitly before 2026-08-23, so a four-field line means today
# what it meant yesterday.
#
# It exists because node 3 arrived as `debian3`, not `debian1`, and the
# alternative (create a matching account on every machine) is a dead end: the
# eventual Claude Skill has to run on other organisations' hardware, where the
# usernames will not match ours either. Parameterising is the thing that gets
# built sooner or later; node 3 is the cheapest place to find what breaks.
# See docs/FINDINGS.md F53.
#
# NOTE this is the ADMIN/SSH login only. It is NOT the service account:
# `rpc-server@.service` runs as `cluster` (created by setup.sh) on every node
# and is deliberately unaffected by the login name. `llama-server@.service`
# DOES need it, and gets it through a per-node systemd drop-in written by
# cluster/install-services.sh -- systemd does not expand variables in `User=`,
# so an EnvironmentFile cannot carry it.
#
# Node 1 is the master and also runs a worker rpc-server.

NODES=(
  "node1 10.10.0.34 128709 4"
  "node2 10.10.0.39 128709 4"
  # Node 3's RAM is genuinely 2 MB under nodes 1/2 (`free -m` = 128707). Measured,
  # not copied -- F29's lesson. Its admin login is `debian3`, hence the 5th field.
  "node3 10.10.0.40 128707 4 debian3"
  # --- nodes 4-7: fill in as each is provisioned. Do NOT guess these values. ---
  # "node4 10.10.0.XX ????? ? [ssh-user]"
  # "node5 10.10.0.XX ????? ? [ssh-user]"
  # "node6 10.10.0.XX ????? ? [ssh-user]"
  # "node7 10.10.0.XX ????? ? [ssh-user]"
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-node SSH login: default and accessors.
#
# Everything that talks to a node over SSH must go through node_target()/
# node_target_for() rather than using the bare IP. A bare `ssh "$IP"` resolves
# to the CALLER's login name, which is right for nodes 1-2 by coincidence and
# wrong for any node whose account is named differently.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_SSH_USER_DEFAULT="${NODE_SSH_USER_DEFAULT:-$(id -un)}"

# node_user   <entry>        -> login name for that NODES entry
# node_target <entry>        -> "user@ip", ready for ssh/scp/rsync
# node_target_for <name|ip>  -> same, looked up by hostname or IP
# shellcheck disable=SC2086
node_user()   { set -- $1; printf '%s\n' "${5:-$NODE_SSH_USER_DEFAULT}"; }
# shellcheck disable=SC2086
node_target() { set -- $1; printf '%s@%s\n' "${5:-$NODE_SSH_USER_DEFAULT}" "$2"; }
node_target_for() {
  local q="$1" e
  for e in "${NODES[@]:-}"; do
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    set -- $e
    if [ "$1" = "$q" ] || [ "$2" = "$q" ]; then
      printf '%s@%s\n' "${5:-$NODE_SSH_USER_DEFAULT}" "$2"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  # Unknown host: fall back to the coordinator's login rather than failing, so
  # an ad-hoc IP still works the way it did before this field existed.
  printf '%s@%s\n' "$NODE_SSH_USER_DEFAULT" "$q"
}

RPC_PORT=50052
MASTER_IP=10.10.0.34

# Node 1's RAM_MB was 125629 until 2026-08-17. That value is NOT what `free -m`
# reports and its origin is unknown; both nodes measure 128709 MB (MemTotal
# 131798676 kB). Corrected, because RAM_MB sets --tensor-split ratios and a
# 2.4% skew between physically identical nodes would misweight the split.
#
# INFERENCE ENDPOINTS are not the same list as RPC endpoints. Under the
# replicated topology (R independent llama-servers) Missing Link must fan out
# across these; under sharding they would be one coordinator. Kept separate
# deliberately -- see STATUS.md task 2.
INFERENCE_ENDPOINTS=(
  "http://10.10.0.34:8080"
  "http://10.10.0.39:8080"
  # node3 is joined but has no model on disk yet -- adding it here before a
  # llama-server actually answers would just park a Missing Link worker in
  # permanent backoff. Add when the model lands.
  # "http://10.10.0.40:8080"
)

# Missing Link runs on the COORDINATOR ONLY, so it is listed explicitly rather
# than derived from NODES. `cluster/llama-watchdog.sh` watches these; an empty
# list simply means "not deployed here".
#
# It is watched by a DIFFERENT predicate from llama-server, deliberately. An
# async queue that idles when there is nothing to do is healthy, so the
# CPU-progress signal that identifies a wedged llama-server would be nonsense
# here. See the predicate notes in cluster/llama-watchdog.sh.
MISSING_LINK_ENDPOINTS=(
  "http://10.10.0.34:8000"
)

# Measured per node -- see docs/measurements.md. F12: memory bandwidth, not RAM
# capacity, sets generation speed, and core count is a BANDWIDTH spec.
#   STREAM triad peak, node1: 28.2 GB/s (2026-08-12), 28.4 GB/s (2026-08-17 re-run)
#   STREAM triad peak, node2: 27.9 GB/s (2026-08-17)
# Node 2 is within 1.8% of node 1 and has an identical DIMM layout
# (4 x 32 GB DDR4-2400, all four channels). Treat the fleet as homogeneous in
# bandwidth for now; --tensor-split by RAM is therefore also correct by
# bandwidth. Re-check per node -- this is an empirical result, not a guarantee.
#
# LINK SPEED IS 100 Mb/s, NOT GIGABIT (measured 93.8 Mbit/s = 11.7 MB/s).
# Both NICs support and advertise 1000baseT/Full, so the cap is the cable or
# switch port, not the hardware. This matters: it makes peer-to-peer model
# pull SLOWER than downloading from HuggingFace (21 MB/s measured, F23).
